{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AyNYdvocVncXXt941eMFcm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: neurico-host-local-llm-fallback\n3\tdescription: \"On the shared /data neurico host, API keys may be absent; GPUs are shared/contended; vLLM recipe, cached models, and pitfalls (pkill self-kill, cudnn clash, prompt_logprobs OOM)\"\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: reference\n7\t  originSessionId: e7324b18-29e9-4db0-ae5a-a9f0cd196a49\n8\t  modified: 2026-10-16T18:23:14.513Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tObserved 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-16 in neurico research workspaces on this host:\n12\t- OPENROUTER_KEY / OPENAI keys were NOT set in the session env (`~/.flamebird/neurico/.env` has it commented out). Resource-finder stage may be skipped (no papers/, datasets/, code/).\n13\t- Local models + datasets cached in `/data/transformers/hub` (HF_HOME=/data/transformers): Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3,7,14}B-Instruct safetensors, Qwen3/3.5 family, Llama-3.1-8B, GGUFs; datasets incl. glue(sst2 only), stanfordnlp/sst2, samsum, ag_news, boolq, mmlu. huggingface.co is reachable for extra downloads (curl resolve/main URLs).\n14\t- 2026-10-16: 4x RTX A6000 (48GB) present but shared — other users' jobs held ~42GB on 3 GPUs and intermittently grabbed the 4th. Approach that worked: vLLM 0.31 (`uv add \"vllm>=0.10\"`; unpinned `uv add vllm` resolved an ancient 0.4 build), small `gpu_memory_utilization` (~0.11 ≈ 5GB) for ≤1.5B models on the leftover slices, plus a bash wrapper that polls `nvidia-smi` free memory and retries launch. vLLM checks free mem ≥ util×total at startup.\n15\t- 2026-10-14 (no GPU then): llama.cpp multi-server CPU fallback worked; `transformers` CPU generation much slower.\n16\t- Pitfalls: `pkill -f <pattern>` inside a Bash call whose text contains the pattern kills the calling shell (find PIDs, kill by PID separately). Leftover `nvidia-cudnn-cu12` 8.x from a failed install breaks flashinfer (\"Found 2 libcudnn.so.x\") — `uv pip uninstall nvidia-cudnn-cu12`. vLLM `prompt_logprobs` materialises full-vocab logits → OOM on small slices; cap `max_num_batched_tokens` (~1024). Don't edit a bash script while a running loop is executing it.\n17\t- Label-restricted log-probs in vLLM: `logprobs_mode=\"processed_logprobs\"` + `allowed_token_ids=label_ids` returns log-softmax renormalised over the labels.\n18\t\n19\tUpdate 2026-10-16 (inter-model-agreement workspace): 4×A6000 present but shared — other neurico sessions keep each GPU at ~6–10 GB free, fluctuating second to second. What worked:\n20\t- `uv venv --python 3.12` (default 3.14 breaks vLLM); pyproject needs `requires-python=\"==3.12.*\"` and `[tool.uv] package=false`.\n21\t- vLLM 0.31: no bitsandbytes; set `VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0` (JIT build fails); `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`.\n22\t- Use pre-quantized 4-bit AWQ/GPTQ checkpoints (~5.5 GB for 8B): Qwen/*-AWQ, hugging-quants Llama-3.1/gemma-2 AWQ-INT4, tiiuae/Falcon3-7B-Instruct-AWQ, solidrust Mistral AWQ, kaitchup Olmo-3/Phi-3 GPTQ.\n23\t- Cap gpu_memory_utilization to ~12.5 GB and retry on startup OOM via a scheduler loop; max_model_len 2560, max_num_seqs 64.\n24\t- Gated models in /data/transformers/hub (meta-llama Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, google/gemma-7b-it) have weights but no config/tokenizer → unusable; unsloth mirror of Llama-3.1 is complete. Disk /data is ~100% full.\n25\t\n26\tUpdate 2026-10-16 (few-shot-ordering workspace, 2nd session): a \"Begin research execution\" prompt can land on a workspace where a prior session already finished most work (REPORT.md exists, background jobs killed at session end). Check logs/*.status and REPORT first and resume only the interrupted pieces rather than restarting. Monitor `tail -n0 -F` on status files missed an append; poll status files directly instead. Qwen2.5-14B bf16 (~30 GB) won't fit when GPUs have ≤23 GB free.\n27\t\n28\tUpdate 2026-10-16 (system_prompt_residual_effects workspace): the neurico runner can kill a session at its 6h limit (return_code 143) and relaunch with the same prompt while detached background jobs (e.g. `run_all.sh`) keep running. On start, check for existing planning.md/REPORT.md, `*_PLACEHOLDER` markers, `.neurico/pipeline_*.json`, `*.partial` checkpoints and live PIDs before redoing anything; usually you only need to wait for the jobs and re-run the scoring/analysis.\n29\t\n30\tUpdate 2026-10-19 (instruction-length workspace): GPU free memory can drop to ≤5 GB on *all* 4 GPUs for hours (other users' jobs) — run the biggest/most important models FIRST while a GPU is free, and run small models (≤1.5B, ~5 GB) in parallel on leftover slices. Qwen2.5-14B bf16 at gpu_mem 42 GB got only ~1.2 GB KV (vLLM counted ~9 GB \"non-torch\") → 80 min for 6k prompts; prefer Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-AWQ (now cached) when speed matters. Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct-AWQ is now cached too. Foreground Bash polls cap at 10 min — use run_in_background until-loops on a status file. `pgrep -f run_wait.sh` also matches other workspaces' identically named scripts — check `ps -o cmd` before killing.\n31\t\n32\tUpdate 2026-10-19 (benchmark-contamination workspace): GPUs again saturated (~5 GB free on 3 GPUs, GPU2 fluctuating 0–40 GB). Extra lessons:\n33\t- On ~5 GB slices vLLM fails with `CUBLAS_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED` / \"No available memory for cache blocks\" unless `enforce_eager=True`, `max_num_batched_tokens≈512–1024`, `max_num_seqs≤48`, util≈0.09–0.095. 1–1.5B bf16 models and 3B-AWQ fit; 7–8B AWQ need ≥11 GB.\n34\t- Large-vocab models (Gemma 256k) OOM in `prompt_logprobs`; cap batched tokens to 512. Pythia needs max_model_len ≤ 2048. kaitchup Phi-3 GPTQ: pass trust_remote_code=False (else offline fetch of remote code fails). google/gemma-3-1b-it snapshot lacks tokenizer → unusable.\n35\t- LoRA (r=64, bs=2, len 320) of a 1.5B model trains in a 5 GB slice (~5–8 min/epoch for ~2.9k docs).\n36\t- `pgrep -f \"<pattern>\"` inside a wait loop matches the loop's own bash command line → use `pgrep -f \"[x]pattern\"` bracket trick.\n37\t- Qwen2.5-3B as a yes/no paraphrase-equivalence judge rejected ~90% of good paraphrases; embedding cosine (all-mpnet-base-v2, cached) worked better.\n38\t- A retry loop's waiting must not count as failed tries (wrote gpu_run_wait.sh) — otherwise jobs give up while waiting for memory.\n39\t\n40\tUpdate 2026-10-19 (decoding_strategy_sensitivity workspace, 11 models × 132k gens, vLLM 0.32):\n41\t- Killing a generate.py parent leaves its `VLLM::EngineCore` child orphaned (ppid 1) holding GPU memory — kill the EngineCore PID too.\n42\t- Size budgets by KV/token, not weights: Phi-3-mini (no GQA) at 6 GB had a 2.2k-token KV cache → ~1 seq concurrency, useless. Olmo-3-7B is also KV-heavy.\n43\t- A small idle slice (5–6 GB on a GPU at 0% util) beat a big slice on a GPU at 100% util shared with 4 jobs (Phi-3 GSM8K 12 min vs 40–80 min for 7B models on the busy GPU). Check `utilization.gpu`, not just free memory.\n44\t- In generate scripts, size gpu_memory_utilization from `torch.cuda.mem_get_info()` minus ~1.2 GB (the parent's CUDA context counts), and bail if it's below ~75% of budget.\n45\t- `trust_remote_code=True` + HF_HUB_OFFLINE fails for checkpoints whose config auto_map points to a Hub repo (kaitchup Phi-3 GPTQ); use False (native arch).\n46\t- Qwen3-8B-AWQ (enable_thinking=False) works as a ~9 GB LLM judge: 17.6k rubric judgments in ~45 min, 0 parse failures.\n47\t\n48\tUpdate 2026-10-20 (tokenizer_fertility_bias workspace, encoder fine-tuning on ~5 GB slices):\n49\t- No resource-finder output again; HF datasets with loading scripts (daily_dialog, banking77) fail — use parquet mirrors (`/api/datasets/<id>/parquet`) or the original CSV URLs in the script.\n50\t- 110M–180M encoders fine-tune fine in 3–5 GB (bs32, len128, bf16 autocast, ~1 min/run). ByT5-small needs ~10 GB even on short sentences and >1 h per 256-word doc run under contention, and collapsed to majority class with Adafactor lr 1e-3 / 3 epochs — budget it as infeasible on shared slices. CANINE also collapsed on short docs in 4/7 runs.\n51\t- Self-kill gotcha, bracket variant: `pgrep -f \"[r]un_queue.py ../logs/jobs_long\"` still killed the calling shell because the same Bash command later contained the literal text `run_queue.py ../logs/jobs_long.txt`. Find PIDs in one call, kill in a separate call.\n52\t- statsmodels formula GLM with a bool outcome column models P(False) (patsy treats it as categorical) → flipped signs; cast to int.\n53\t- Byte-level BPE (GPT-2/Llama/Qwen) offset_mapping includes the leading space; map tokens to words by the end offset.\n54\t\n55\tUpdate 2026-10-20 (icl_label_bias workspace): GLUE in the cache has only sst2 — RTE/MRPC parquet downloads directly from `huggingface.co/datasets/nyu-mll/glue/resolve/main/<task>/<split>-00000-of-00001.parquet`. One GPU was fully free at session start and got taken within ~5 min, so launch the biggest model immediately. On a busy shared GPU, 7–8B AWQ at ~8.6 GB budget (eager) ran ~10k 600-token prompts in ~75 min; 14B bf16 took 74 min. Two wait-loops seeing the same free GPU simultaneously race and OOM — stagger launches (~2 min). Bootstrap ECE CIs via pd.concat per resample were >10 min; per-item bin sufficient statistics × multinomial weights was seconds.\n56\t\n57\tUpdate 2026-10-20 (quantization_failure_modes workspace, 7 models × 7 RTN levels, vLLM 0.32):\n58\t- In-place weight edits inside vLLM work: `llm.collective_rpc(fn)` with `VLLM_ALLOW_INSECURE_SERIALIZATION=1`, fn(self) touches `self.model_runner.model`; keep a CPU backup in the worker and quantize row-chunks (full-matrix fp32 temps OOM on slices). Disable prefix caching.\n59\t- Don't call `torch.cuda.mem_get_info()` in the vLLM parent — its CUDA context eats ~0.8 GB on GPU0 and startup then fails the free-memory check; get total via nvidia-smi.\n60\t- GPUs 0/1 top out at exactly 5125 MiB free (other users' 43 GB jobs) → budget ≤4.4 GB per slice. Multi-GPU TP rarely gets simultaneous slices; a queue with alternative configs (\"1:9.5,2:4.4\") was what worked. TP shards must keep quant groups aligned (Qwen2.5-3B intermediate 11008/4 not divisible by 128).\n61\t- HF `device_map` sharding needs `accelerate` installed (not pulled in by vllm).\n62\t- Self-kill again: `pkill -f`/`grep '[x]pat'` still matches if the SAME command line also contains the pattern elsewhere (e.g. a later nohup). Kill by PID in a separate call.\n63\t- Contended-GPU timings: 7–8B bf16 on a 20 GB slice ~2–8 min/level for 1.6k prompts once GPU2 freed (~17:00–19:00); Phi-3 (KV-heavy) on 13 GB of a 100%-util GPU took 33 min.\n64\t\n65\tUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_threshold workspace): GPUs saturated all session (≤6 GB free, 7–8B AWQ at 8 GB never started in 40+ tries). The user's own long-lived `vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --port 8011` (cascade venv, GPU3) was idle and usable via `/v1/completions` (raw prompt + stop strings; `stop_reason` returned) — check `curl 127.0.0.1:8011/v1/models` and `/metrics` num_requests_running before using; it gave the only 7B+ model and an LLM judge. For multi-round generation against a server, run each item's rounds in its own thread (synchronous batch rounds stall on stragglers: 4 min/round → 13 min total). New vLLM startup failure on contended GPUs: \"Error in memory profiling … other processes release GPU memory\" — just retry. HotpotQA distractor prompts reach ~2.4k tokens; use max_model_len ≥4096.\n66\t\n67\tUpdate 2026-10-21 (context_window_decay workspace, long-context 1K–16K prompts):\n68\t- GPUs saturated again from ~13:00 (other neurico sessions + liuxiao TP job); GPU0/1 \"5125 MiB free\" is another job's fluctuating activation memory — launches there fail even when the check passes. A single scheduler (one launch per GPU per 120 s) beats per-job wait loops, which raced each other (4 OOMs at once when GPU2 briefly freed 29 GB).\n69\t- Startup-race fix that worked: in run_model.py, allocate a placeholder `torch.empty(budget+0.5 GB)` immediately, release it when the vLLM EngineCore child maps `libcuda.so` (poll /proc/<child>/maps every 50 ms). Guard with an env flag (HOLD_PARENT) — vLLM spawns EngineCore by re-importing __main__, so the hold code otherwise runs again in the child. Scheduler threshold: budget + 1.3 GB.\n70\t- 16K-token prompts: Qwen2.5-7B-AWQ on a busy shared GPU ≈3k tok/s → 2,880 prompts (18M tokens) took 1h40; budget runs by total prompt tokens. 0.5–1.5B on an idle slice: 5–15 min.\n71\t- `AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ` downloads in <1 min and runs in a ~4 GB slice up to ~9K context. Qwen3-0.6B (non-thinking) answers \"number or name\" questions with a number 95% of the time.\n72\t\n73\tUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_tax workspace, 6+ concurrent neurico sessions): GPUs were ~fully oversubscribed; launches failed ~80% from free-memory races. Lessons:\n74\t- Long multi-round generation (budget forcing, 12 rounds × up to 2k tokens) on a 100%-util shared GPU is very slow: 7B-AWQ forced arm took ~4 h incl. a ~60 min final answer pass (small KV → ~15 concurrent long contexts). Budget for it, or cut rounds/items for ≥7B.\n75\t- My own jobs on the same GPU slow each other badly — don't stack 7B+14B+alt runs on one GPU.\n76\t- Two of my queues polling independently launched on the same GPU in the same second and both failed: use ONE queue. A queue restart loses track of its running children → mark them (EXTERNAL file) or it relaunches duplicates. Stop leftover launch.sh wrappers at session end (they relaunch after you kill the child).\n77\t- Qwen3-4B-AWQ (download ~2.5 GB) works as a 4.4 GB-slice LLM judge: ~1k rubric labels in ~4 min, 0 parse failures, but applies categories loosely — audit samples.\n78\t- pandas 3: groupby().apply drops grouping columns — restore them by index join.\n79\t- Background Bash with run_in_background still caps at 10 min when a timeout isn't given — set timeout explicitly for long waits.\n80\t\n81\tUpdate 2026-10-21 (positional_retrieval_decay workspace): GPUs saturated again by ~6 parallel neurico sessions, each running retry loops that race for the same freed slices. What worked:\n82\t- **Retry engine creation in-process** (poll nvidia-smi, size budget = min(max, free−0.35), retry LLM() after failures) instead of relaunching; build prompts AFTER engine init to shrink the race window.\n83\t- **Work-stealing pool** (src/worker_pool.py): one thread per GPU launches the highest-priority job whose min budget fits; per-tag flock files prevent duplicates; exit code 75 = \"gave up waiting\" (not a failure). Chunked generation with append-only .partial files (whole samples) made kills/resumes safe and let analysis use partial runs.\n84\t- Duplicate-run gotcha: a \"move finished .jsonl back to .partial\" extension script raced a queue that retried the same tag → two writers on one file. Use the lock in every code path.\n85\t- 7–8B AWQ on a GPU shared by ~7 processes: 12–21 min per 720 prompts of avg 3K tokens; ≤3B models: 0.5–3 min. Budget large models at N/3.\n86\t- WikiText-103 test+val gives only ~49 passages of ≥7.5K words (~10K tokens) → a \"16K\" haystack silently capped at ~10K; check ctx_tokens per length.\n87\t- Single-needle retrieval is at ceiling for Qwen2.5 ≥1.5B / Llama-8B / Qwen3-4B up to 10K; near-miss distractors (same name, different type) are the discriminating condition.\n88\t\n89\tUpdate 2026-10-21 (prompt_surface_sensitivity workspace, 10 models × 24k prompts, vLLM 0.32):\n90\t- Shared-GPU startup races were lost ~90% of the time (other sessions fill a freed slice during vLLM's ~20 s startup). What fixed it: TP=1 → `VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0` (in-process engine) + grab memory with a `torch.empty(budget)` tensor as soon as free, and monkeypatch `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker.MemorySnapshot` to free the tensor + `empty_cache()` just before vLLM measures. TP=2 → per-GPU holder subprocesses + `VLLM_WORKER_MULTIPROC_METHOD=fork` so forked workers inherit the patch and SIGKILL their GPU's holder. TP workers create CUDA ctx+NCCL (~0.8 GB) *before* the snapshot, so a 5.0 GB slice can't host TP; need ≥5.6 GB/GPU. Holders must exit when their parent dies (poll getppid), else orphans hog memory. Code: that workspace's src/generate.py, src/gpu_queue.py.\n91\t- Llama-3.x and Gemma-2 chat templates `| trim` message content (trailing whitespace is a no-op); Qwen2.5 keeps it — useful as a free placebo.\n92\t- vLLM greedy decoding is not batch-invariant: byte-identical prompts in different batch positions gave identical long (≤1024-tok) outputs only 23–76% of the time, moving IFEval accuracy by up to ~3 pp; short (≤10-tok) outputs ~99.7% identical.\n93\t- Two GPU queues launching TP jobs onto the same GPU pair race each other — run one queue.\n94\t\n95\tUpdate 2026-10-22 (sampling_temp_misinformation workspace, 7 models × 9 temps × n=10, vLLM 0.32):\n96\t- GPU2 was fully free at 11:20 and ~37 GB of it was taken within 5 min — launch immediately.\n97\t- In-process hold (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0 + torch.empty placeholder + MemorySnapshot subclass patched in both\n98\t  `vllm.utils.mem_utils` and `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker`) started 3B/7B-AWQ jobs on the first try. Re-acquire the hold\n99\t  before each retry, or retries run without it and fail. Pick the GPU (max free) before importing torch, then pin to it.\n100\t- Qwen2.5-3B bf16 at an 8.5 GB budget (non-eager, 4096 batched tokens) → \"No available memory for the cache blocks\".\n101\t  3B-AWQ in 4.4 GB works.\n102\t- SamplingParams(n=10, seed=s) seeds the samples consecutively → requests with seeds s and s+1 share 9/10 streams\n103\t  (common random numbers across conditions). Space per-request seeds ≥ n apart if independence is wanted.\n104\t- GSM8K 512-token CoT × 81 samples × 400 items on a 100%-util shared GPU: 1.5–3 h per ≤3B model; the QA part takes minutes.\n105\t- Paired Wilcoxon on fractional, zero-heavy item-level diffs (sample-mean minus 0/1) contradicts the bootstrap CIs;\n106\t  use a sign-flip permutation test of the mean.\n107\t\n108\tUpdate 2026-10-22 (embedding_drift_finetuning workspace, LoRA fine-tuning 160M–7B on shared GPUs):\n109\t- A fully free GPU (48 GB) at session start was reservable with a tiny holder (`torch.empty(36GB)` + exit on a RELEASE file) while code was written; have the training script touch RELEASE after loading weights to CPU, just before `.cuda()`. But whatever you don't hold gets taken within minutes, and the 7B LoRA run (17 GB) still OOM'd mid-run when co-tenants grew — budget 7B training as infeasible unless a GPU is dedicated. On a GPU at 100% util shared with another user, 7B LoRA bs4×512 ran ~7 s/step; 3B 4.6 s/step; 1.5B 2.9 s/step; checkpoint evals (1.6k probes + 1,070 MCQ items) 4–8.5 min. 0.5B LoRA fits a 5 GB slice (2.4 GB peak, 20 min/3k steps); 1.5B peaks 4.5 GB.\n110\t- \"Idle\" 5–6 GB slices at 0% util are transient: other users' 41–43 GB jobs regrow and OOM you minutes later.\n111\t- Worker queue bug: a worker that retries the first unclaimed job starves smaller jobs if that job never fits → per-job memory need + skip; and when killing a worker loop, its job lock dir is orphaned (rmdir it). Writing reps before accuracy evals means a mid-eval kill leaves a checkpoint with reps but no metrics — filter to steps present in metrics.jsonl.\n112\t- Science gotcha: always log held-out in-domain loss — Pythia-160M LoRA (r64, α128, lr 2e-4) diverged in every domain, and Qwen2.5 base on codeparrot Python showed no in-domain gain; \"drift\" results otherwise get misread.\n113\t\n114\tUpdate 2026-10-23 (repetition_penalty_sideeffects workspace): GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:21 but was taken before vLLM was even installed (~10 min) — the uv install of vllm took >10 min (run it in background immediately). 7B-AWQ at 6.3–8.4 GB never started all session (all GPUs ≤1.5 GB free from 12:40); ≤3B AWQ at 4.2 GB ran fine. The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 had only ~2% KV usage at 24 concurrent requests — use 48–64 concurrency (~6 gen/s for 300-token outputs; LLM-judge ~75–100 calls/min). It accepts `repetition_penalty` and `chat_template_kwargs: {enable_thinking: false}` in /v1/chat/completions. HF `model.generate` with a custom LogitsProcessor (1–1.5B bf16, bs 50, 320 new tokens) took 5–45 s/batch on a shared slice — fine for ablations vLLM can't express.\n115\t\n116\tUpdate 2026-10-23 (batch_size_inference_quality workspace, 10 configs × 1,700 prompts × 7 batch conditions, vLLM 0.32):\n117\t- GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:22 but a sibling session (3 neurico sessions start together at 12:00) took 40 GB within ~1 min — the free GPU at session start is a race you'll usually lose; plan for ~5 GB slices.\n118\t- Killing a job to restart it with different settings loses its slice within seconds (other sessions' wait-loops grab it) — prefer env/file-driven behaviour changes decided at launch; unpinned (no CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES) relaunch with a smaller budget got a slice again.\n119\t- Static-chunk batching in one engine (`llm.generate(chunk_of_B)`) is a clean batch-size manipulation. B=1 eager on a shared GPU: ~50–60 min (0.5–1.5B) for 1,700 prompts incl. 700 × ≤512-token CoT; 3B bf16 B=4 ~2 h → budget B=1 on subsets for ≥3B.\n120\t- Result worth reusing: vLLM bf16 greedy is deterministic for an identical schedule (0 diffs) but batch-dependent: B=1 vs B≥4 changes 47–76% of CoT outputs; fp16 ~5× and fp32 ~12× fewer; divergences start at bf16 top-2 ties (logprob margin 0 or 0.125).\n121\t- Analysis gotcha: iterating `set(a) & set(b)` of string ids gives PYTHONHASHSEED-dependent order → bootstrap CIs not reproducible; sort first.\n122\t\n123\tUpdate 2026-10-23 (system_prompt_saturation workspace, 9 models × 3.6k prompts up to 8k tokens, vLLM 0.32):\n124\t- GPU2 fully free at 12:20; grabbed it with a chunked holder (1 GB tensors, target size read from a control file) — but\n125\t  the 9 GB freed between two of my jobs was taken by other sessions within ~1 min, and later jobs got only 5–13 GB.\n126\t- vLLM memory profiling charges other tenants' growth during startup to *your* \"non-torch\" usage (8B AWQ at 13 GB got\n127\t  1.1 GB KV → startup ValueError). Fix: `LLM(kv_cache_memory_bytes=...)` skips profiling entirely; size the hold as\n128\t  weights + KV + ~1.5 GB.\n129\t- Holder handoff bugs: compute the holder's *actual* size from `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` for its PID, not the\n130\t  target file; and on a 100%-full GPU, CUDA context creation raises `torch.AcceleratorError` (a RuntimeError), not\n131\t  OutOfMemoryError — catch both and wait for the holder to free before touching CUDA.\n132\t- Per-output-file `fcntl.flock` in generate.py (print \"LOCKED … 0 to do\" and exit 0) made several queues on different\n133\t  GPUs safe against duplicate writers.\n134\t- Timings on a GPU shared by ~8 processes: 8B AWQ with a 3.5 GB KV (28k tokens) needed 3 h for 3.6k prompts (1.2k of\n135\t  them 1–8k tokens); 14B AWQ with 13.6k-token KV couldn't finish long prompts at all. Short-prompt experiments for\n136\t  big models first, long-context ones only if KV ≥ ~20k tokens.\n137\t- Greedy re-run of Qwen2.5-1.5B on a different GPU/KV size: 13% byte-identical texts but rule-check agreement 93.7%\n138\t  and mean metric within 0.07 pp.\n139\t\n140\tUpdate 2026-10-26 (lora_rank_blind_spots workspace, ~170 LoRA train+eval jobs of Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B, HF+peft, no vLLM):\n141\t- GPUs had 0.5–6 GB free at 10:40, then ~25–43 GB freed on 3 GPUs at ~11:24 for ~10 min. A queue that only launches one job per GPU every 75 s wastes such a window; LAUNCH_GAP≈20–30 s filled it.\n142\t- Without a concurrency cap my queue launched 52 jobs → load avg 60 on 32 cores, each job 5× slower. Cap running jobs (~15–20) and set OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 per job.\n143\t- Mid-run OOMs from co-tenants: reserving memory at start (`torch.empty(RESERVE_GB)`, then del → it stays in PyTorch's cache) and saving the LoRA adapter right after training (so eval-only retries are possible) stopped most of the lost work. Real peak for a 0.5B LoRA job was 2.3–4.8 GB, more than estimated. HF generate prefill on 32 prompts × ~50 tokens still allocates ~0.5 GB of full-vocab logits.\n144\t- After gradient_checkpointing_disable(), pass use_cache=True to generate explicitly.\n145\t- Timings under contention: 0.5B LoRA, 250 steps, bs16 → 1.5 min (short classification inputs, idle GPU) up to 25–30 min (GSM8K/code, ~20 co-running jobs).\n146\t\n147\tUpdate 2026-10-26 (greedy_vs_nucleus_crossover workspace, 6 models × 6 tasks × 6 decoding conds, ~144k gens):\n148\t- GPUs again ~4 GB free per GPU at 0% util. Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3}B-Instruct-AWQ all ran in ≤3.7 GB slices (weights + kv 0.6–0.8 GB + ~1.0 GB overhead, `kv_cache_memory_bytes`, enforce_eager) and started first try; 7B-AWQ (7.4 GB) got a slice after ~35 min of waiting.\n149\t- The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 was idle again: 8.1k chat requests (n=3, up to 512 tok) in ~25 min at 48 threads; single-token logprob judge (max_tokens=1, top_logprobs=20, probability-weighted 1–9) did 7.2k ratings in ~5–7 min. Digit mass ≈1.0.\n150\t- Pooled-sample entropy conflates model errors with answer multiplicity (small models' wrong TriviaQA answers look \"high entropy\"); restrict to valid outputs for a gold-entropy proxy.\n151\t- CPU sentence-embedding of ~40k texts under load avg ~50 took ~15 min; cache embeddings to disk for re-runs.\n152\t\n153\tUpdate 2026-10-26 (kv_cache_compression_artifacts workspace, HF-transformers 5.20 custom-attention work): no API key; Qwen3-8B server on :8011 again available as judge. GPUs at ~4 GB free at 10:40, then 0/1/2 freed to ~40 GB at ~11:24 for ~5 min (grab immediately; 7B bf16 at 16 GB later lost its slot during a worker hand-off — never kill a placed worker to \"restart with other args\"). Lessons:\n154\t- Cache-policy experiments without vLLM: register a custom fn via `transformers.AttentionInterface`, run `model.model(..., use_cache=False, position_ids=...)` and keep your own KV store inside the fn (no mask needed: custom names skip HF mask creation). ~1 s/item for 1.5B at 6k ctx, 128-token chunks.\n155\t- place_model pattern: load to CPU, poll nvidia-smi, `.cuda()` + warm-up forward + reserve; on failure `os._exit(75)` and relaunch from a bash loop (a failed in-process attempt leaves a 0.4 GB CUDA ctx pinned on that GPU).\n156\t- Teacher-forced log_softmax over 256×152k vocab OOM'd a 4 GB slice mid-job → job lock orphaned with 4/150 rows. After a grid, check every output file's row count.\n157\t- Qwen2.5 naive per-token 4-bit KV quant is catastrophic (key outlier channels); KIVI per-channel keys fine. Llama-3.2 fine either way.\n158\t- LLM-judge for contradictions: free-form \"list contradictions\" with Qwen3-8B over-reports; slot extraction + deterministic comparison worked.\n159\t\n160\tUpdate 2026-10-27 (tokenizer_merge_order workspace): **numpy ≥2 allocations >4 MB hang indefinitely** (100% CPU, e.g. `np.ones(5M uint16)`, `arr.astype(int64)`) because numpy madvises hugepages and THP defrag=madvise stalls on this host. Fix: `export NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0` for every python process (bytearray/torch allocs were fine). py-spy can't attach (no ptrace); use `python -X faulthandler` + `faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(40)`. GPUs 0–2 showed 48 GB free at 11:03 and were ~3 GB free by 11:08; GPU3 sat at 6.1 GB free / 0% util. Tiny-LM pretraining (23M params, ctx 512, bf16) ≈ 300k tok/s on an idle slice, ~2.5 GB at micro-batch 16.\n161\t\n162\tUpdate 2026-10-27 (gradient_masking_safety workspace, 14 models 0.5–32B × 5.4k prompts, vLLM 0.32): 3 GPUs were fully free at 11:03 and taken within ~10 min; grabbing 40 GB immediately with a 1-GB-chunk holder paid off. What worked best:\n163\t- ONE long-lived in-process worker (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0) that runs all models sequentially and never returns memory to the driver: disable `torch.cuda.empty_cache`/`torch.accelerator.empty_cache`, drop placeholder chunks into PyTorch's cache at `MemorySnapshot.measure`, and add `memory_reserved()-memory_allocated()` to the snapshot's free_memory. Releasing to the driver at measure time lost ~9 GB to other tenants during weight loading (OOM). Queue entries `task=<fn>:<arg>` dispatched via importlib.reload(tasks) let code change without restarting (a restart = handoff race). Handoff to a new worker: TAKEOVER_PID tight-loop grab after the old pid exits kept 26/26 GB.\n164\t- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server intermittently returned CORRUPTED logprobs (sampled token != top-logprob argmax, top-k mass ~1e-6); it looked like a judge \"safety reflex\" (refusals scored as compliance, 9/37 accuracy). Validate every logprob judgment (argmax==sampled token, mass>=0.5, retry, fall back to text). With validation, ~10–19 judgments/s.\n165\t- Judge design that validated (37 hand labels): reply-only \"does the assistant decline?\" (34/37) + request+reply \"does the reply contain content that carries out the request?\" (35/37). cais/HarmBench-Mistral-7b-val-cls weights downloaded to the cache (~14 GB, 3 min).\n166\t- Timings on the shared A6000 (26 GB slice): ≤1.5B 2–4 min, 3B ~18 min, Phi-3 35 min, 7–9B 10–22 min, 14B-AWQ 8 min, 32B-AWQ 40 min per ~4–5k prompts (≤256 tokens), varying with contention.\n167\t\n168\tUpdate 2026-10-29 (fewshot_example_contamination workspace, 8 models × 3,150 gens, vLLM 0.32): load avg 230–350; `ps aux`/`pgrep -af` hang on /proc of stuck processes — use `pgrep -x uv -u $USER` + /proc/<pid>/{stat,wchan,cwd} with `timeout`.\n169\t- **uv install blocked for 10+ min**: 3 sibling sessions' `uv add` all waited on the shared ~/.cache/uv lock (futex) and copies cache(/ root FS)→/data. Fix: `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache uv sync` (same FS → hardlinks; fresh download of vllm stack ~3 min, \"Installed 215 packages in 7.8s\"). A killed uv became a zombie whose stuck threads still held `.venv/.lock` (find holder via `grep :<inode> /proc/locks`) → `mv .venv .venv_stuck_old` and recreate.\n170\t- No-dep server client: `/usr/bin/python3 -I` + urllib works against :8011 while the venv installs.\n171\t- Lane-script bug: `grep -q \"0 trials to do\"` matches \"3150 trials to do\" → failed launches counted as done. Anchor on `^<tag>: 0 trials to do`.\n172\t- GPU0 was fully free 13:15–13:40; 7B/8B/14B AWQ all started first try with `kv_cache_memory_bytes` + gpu_memory_utilization=(budget+0.2)/48. :8011 Qwen3-8B did 3,150 short gens in ~25 min when idle, but crawled (~20/min) once other users loaded it to 110 concurrent requests.\n173\t\n174\tUpdate 2026-10-29 (context_window_cliff workspace, long-context 1K–32K probes, vLLM 0.32): no API key again; `uv add vllm` took ~15 min (slow copy, disk 98%). :8011 Qwen3-8B server was shared with another session (~30–45 running reqs) yet did 1,548 prompts of avg 12K tokens in ~110 min at 16 threads. All my local jobs auto-landed on GPU0 (100% util) → 7B-AWQ ~3.9 prompts/min at 16K avg; moving 1.5B/3B AWQ to the *idle* GPU1 4.2 GB slice cut ETA ~8× (actual use ≈ weights+kv+0.5 GB, so OVERHEAD=0.5, MARGIN=0.25 fits). Shuffling prompt order before running makes any partial run a uniform subsample — kill slow jobs at the deadline without losing design balance. Science gotcha: Phi-3-mini-4k (kaitchup GPTQ) has sliding_window 2047 honoured by vLLM → retrieval of facts >2K tokens from the question ≈0% (looks like a \"length cliff\").\n175\t\n176\tUpdate 2026-10-29 (invisible_formatting_effects workspace, 11 models × 20k prompts, vLLM 0.32): `uv add vllm` took ~20 min (load avg 230–340, slow file copy) — start it first thing. GPU0 briefly had 18 GB free at 13:26 and then 20 GB at 14:49 — launching a worker immediately both times paid off. What worked:\n177\t- **Worker hand-over without losing the slice** (to change code, queue order or registry): the new worker gets `TAKEOVER_PID=<old>`, finishes its imports, writes a ready file, busy-waits until /proc/<old> disappears, then creates its CUDA context and grabs 512 MB chunks. Kill the old PID in a *separate* call once the ready file exists. This kept 5/5 GB and 15/15 GB. It was used to move 14B code generation behind 7B/8B, triggered automatically by a log line (\"cls 14400/14400\").\n178\t- Old workers re-read the shared queue file but keep their imported MODELS registry → adding new keys to that queue would KeyError them. Give new workers their own queue file.\n179\t- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server was fast at first (~3.5k short chat requests in 90 s), then other users loaded it (48 running + 63 waiting), and the last ~2.5k 512-token code requests took ~2 h.\n180\t- Re-running the same checkpoint in a separate engine (same prompts, same shuffle) reproduced 19,968/19,968 outputs byte-identically; a byte-identical prompt at a different batch position changed 2–21% of 512-token code outputs but ~0.02% of short labels.\n181\t- Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ on a 15 GB slice of a 100%-util GPU: ~5 min per 1.5k short prompts, ~13 min per 1.5k 512-token code prompts.\n182\t\n183\tUpdate 2026-10-30 (system_prompt_interference workspace, 7 models × 5.1k prompts, vLLM 0.32): GPU0 was fully free at 13:17 and taken (41 GB) within seconds of my holder starting — I got ~6 GB, enough for ≤3B AWQ (~3 min/model for 5k short prompts). :8011 Qwen3-8B idle: 5.1k chats (≤512 tok) in ~10 min at 48 threads. A 9.3 GB-free poller caught a 10 GB slice for 7B-AWQ ~15 min in.\n184\t- **Never `mv` a results file a live worker is appending to** — /tmp is another FS, so the worker's later writes went to an unlinked inode (lost 3.1k 7B gens). Copy (`cp`) for scoring snapshots.\n185\t- TAKEOVER_PID hand-over lost the slice 2/2 times on GPU0 today (other tenant grows into freed memory within ms); worked earlier on GPU0 from a plain holder. Treat hand-over as risky when the GPU's co-tenant is actively allocating.\n186\t- Shuffled prompt order made the partial 7B run a uniform subsample, but paired designs (first/last, base/repeat) lose most pairs (p²) — for paired designs shuffle at the *pair/group* level instead.\n187\t\n188\tUpdate 2026-10-30 (system_prompt_interference, paper stage): before writing a paper, re-audit REPORT claims against raw gens; \"no false negatives\" was wrong (P_ahoy `_strip_title` missed same-line `<<Title>> Ahoy`, 509 false fails). neurips_2025.sty with `[final]` alone leaves `\\@trackname` undefined (add `\\providecommand` after the package line); math.tex has no `\\Tabref`. paper_examples/ held only a README.\n189\t\n190\tUpdate 2026-10-30 (embedding_collapse_repetition workspace, 18 models frozen embeddings, HF only, no vLLM): stack (torch/transformers/sentence-transformers, ~2 min with `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache`; delete that 3.8 GB cache afterwards). GPU0 showed 48 GB free at 13:17 and was gone within ~8 min; feature extraction fits ~3–4 GB slices and took ~1–5 min per model × 26k short inputs.\n191\t- Cache gaps: gpt2-xl, facebook/opt-125m and thenlper/gte-small have incomplete/split snapshots; `snapshot_download` a config-only snapshot then link weights from the other snapshot into a private folder rather than editing the shared cache.\n192\t- `echo \"$(date) rc=$?\"` reports date's status, not the job's: capture `rc=$?` on its own line first (this hid failures as rc=0). Overwriting a running bash script with `cat >` is unsafe; kill the loop first.\n193\t- Foreground Bash calls that exceed their timeout get moved to background and are killed 10 min later; use nohup for long analysis.\n194\t- Science: mean-pooled \"collapse\" under repetition can be pure dilution. Always pool over original tokens only as a check, keep a length-matched non-repetitive control, and check clean top-PC share (GPT-2 final layer 48%, ModernBERT depth .75 84%) before trusting erank %.\n195\t\n196\tUpdate 2026-10-30 (temperature_calibration_gaps workspace, 12 runs × 6,204 cells × 4 samples, vLLM 0.32): GPU0 48 GB free at 13:17; an existing workspace venv's python can run a chunked holder within seconds while your own `uv add vllm` runs (~4 min with UV_CACHE_DIR local, `.uv_cache` then deletable).\n197\t- The \"hand placeholders to vLLM via PyTorch's cache\" trick failed for large allocations here (14B bf16, 7B bf16): 512 MB cached blocks can't serve multi-GB KV/embedding allocations → OOM with GBs \"reserved but unallocated\"; `expandable_segments` didn't fix it. What worked: pop w+1 GB of placeholders into the cache at MemorySnapshot.measure, wrap `gw.Worker.initialize_from_config` to release the rest with the real empty_cache just before KV allocation (v0.32 uses vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_runner.py, so patching gpu_model_runner silently does nothing), then refill an activation margin. Use enforce_eager=True. Set gpu_memory_utilization=(w+0.3)/47.4 or the startup check can fail by 0.02 GB.\n198\t- A second engine in one process sometimes dies with \"CUDA error: invalid argument\". Safest is one model per process: finishing worker spawns successor (TAKEOVER_PID=self), waits for its per-PID ready file, exits. A shared `takeover_ready` filename is ambiguous with several workers — I acted on the wrong worker's file.\n199\t- Idle small slices beat big busy ones by >10×: 3B AWQ on a 5.5 GB slice of GPU3 did 6.2k cells in ~8 min vs 7B on 100%-util GPU0 ~45 min. Check GPU1/3 slices first.\n200\t- `mv`/`ls` on logs can stall minutes under load (showed `-?????????`). Grepping big worker logs in a poll loop stalled my pipeline: grep only current small logs. Don't reuse a log glob matching old failed-run lines.\n201\t- CUDA allocator OOM spam grew one log to 14 MB; a tight grab() retry loop writes thousands of lines.\n202\t- TAKEOVER_PID=1 waits forever; use a nonexistent PID for \"no predecessor\".\n203\t- Science: mpnet embeddings of word salad collapse together (semantic diversity falls above the coherence cliff, an artefact); the Qwen3-8B judge floors at ~3 on a 1–9 scale even for gibberish → use share ≥5. Mistral-7B AWQ (solidrust) scored only .27 on HumanEval while 0.72 on TriviaQA; not an import artefact.\n204\t- analyze.py with 2,000 bootstraps × 12 models × many metrics takes ~10 min under load; run in background.\n205\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b0894c3-d91e-45a5-b742-1e1609c0e675","uuid":"d23dfdee-c33e-4d3b-9e8f-7354a1ada64f","timestamp":"2026-10-31T01:58:19.503Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/summereunann/.claude/projects/-data/memory/neurico-host-local-llm-fallback.md","content":"---\nname: neurico-host-local-llm-fallback\ndescription: \"On the shared /data neurico host, API keys may be absent; GPUs are shared/contended; vLLM recipe, cached models, and pitfalls (pkill self-kill, cudnn clash, prompt_logprobs OOM)\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: e7324b18-29e9-4db0-ae5a-a9f0cd196a49\n  modified: 2026-10-16T18:23:14.513Z\n---\n\nObserved 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-16 in neurico research workspaces on this host:\n- OPENROUTER_KEY / OPENAI keys were NOT set in the session env (`~/.flamebird/neurico/.env` has it commented out). Resource-finder stage may be skipped (no papers/, datasets/, code/).\n- Local models + datasets cached in `/data/transformers/hub` (HF_HOME=/data/transformers): Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3,7,14}B-Instruct safetensors, Qwen3/3.5 family, Llama-3.1-8B, GGUFs; datasets incl. glue(sst2 only), stanfordnlp/sst2, samsum, ag_news, boolq, mmlu. huggingface.co is reachable for extra downloads (curl resolve/main URLs).\n- 2026-10-16: 4x RTX A6000 (48GB) present but shared — other users' jobs held ~42GB on 3 GPUs and intermittently grabbed the 4th. Approach that worked: vLLM 0.31 (`uv add \"vllm>=0.10\"`; unpinned `uv add vllm` resolved an ancient 0.4 build), small `gpu_memory_utilization` (~0.11 ≈ 5GB) for ≤1.5B models on the leftover slices, plus a bash wrapper that polls `nvidia-smi` free memory and retries launch. vLLM checks free mem ≥ util×total at startup.\n- 2026-10-14 (no GPU then): llama.cpp multi-server CPU fallback worked; `transformers` CPU generation much slower.\n- Pitfalls: `pkill -f <pattern>` inside a Bash call whose text contains the pattern kills the calling shell (find PIDs, kill by PID separately). Leftover `nvidia-cudnn-cu12` 8.x from a failed install breaks flashinfer (\"Found 2 libcudnn.so.x\") — `uv pip uninstall nvidia-cudnn-cu12`. vLLM `prompt_logprobs` materialises full-vocab logits → OOM on small slices; cap `max_num_batched_tokens` (~1024). Don't edit a bash script while a running loop is executing it.\n- Label-restricted log-probs in vLLM: `logprobs_mode=\"processed_logprobs\"` + `allowed_token_ids=label_ids` returns log-softmax renormalised over the labels.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-16 (inter-model-agreement workspace): 4×A6000 present but shared — other neurico sessions keep each GPU at ~6–10 GB free, fluctuating second to second. What worked:\n- `uv venv --python 3.12` (default 3.14 breaks vLLM); pyproject needs `requires-python=\"==3.12.*\"` and `[tool.uv] package=false`.\n- vLLM 0.31: no bitsandbytes; set `VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0` (JIT build fails); `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`.\n- Use pre-quantized 4-bit AWQ/GPTQ checkpoints (~5.5 GB for 8B): Qwen/*-AWQ, hugging-quants Llama-3.1/gemma-2 AWQ-INT4, tiiuae/Falcon3-7B-Instruct-AWQ, solidrust Mistral AWQ, kaitchup Olmo-3/Phi-3 GPTQ.\n- Cap gpu_memory_utilization to ~12.5 GB and retry on startup OOM via a scheduler loop; max_model_len 2560, max_num_seqs 64.\n- Gated models in /data/transformers/hub (meta-llama Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, google/gemma-7b-it) have weights but no config/tokenizer → unusable; unsloth mirror of Llama-3.1 is complete. Disk /data is ~100% full.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-16 (few-shot-ordering workspace, 2nd session): a \"Begin research execution\" prompt can land on a workspace where a prior session already finished most work (REPORT.md exists, background jobs killed at session end). Check logs/*.status and REPORT first and resume only the interrupted pieces rather than restarting. Monitor `tail -n0 -F` on status files missed an append; poll status files directly instead. Qwen2.5-14B bf16 (~30 GB) won't fit when GPUs have ≤23 GB free.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-16 (system_prompt_residual_effects workspace): the neurico runner can kill a session at its 6h limit (return_code 143) and relaunch with the same prompt while detached background jobs (e.g. `run_all.sh`) keep running. On start, check for existing planning.md/REPORT.md, `*_PLACEHOLDER` markers, `.neurico/pipeline_*.json`, `*.partial` checkpoints and live PIDs before redoing anything; usually you only need to wait for the jobs and re-run the scoring/analysis.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-19 (instruction-length workspace): GPU free memory can drop to ≤5 GB on *all* 4 GPUs for hours (other users' jobs) — run the biggest/most important models FIRST while a GPU is free, and run small models (≤1.5B, ~5 GB) in parallel on leftover slices. Qwen2.5-14B bf16 at gpu_mem 42 GB got only ~1.2 GB KV (vLLM counted ~9 GB \"non-torch\") → 80 min for 6k prompts; prefer Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-AWQ (now cached) when speed matters. Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct-AWQ is now cached too. Foreground Bash polls cap at 10 min — use run_in_background until-loops on a status file. `pgrep -f run_wait.sh` also matches other workspaces' identically named scripts — check `ps -o cmd` before killing.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-19 (benchmark-contamination workspace): GPUs again saturated (~5 GB free on 3 GPUs, GPU2 fluctuating 0–40 GB). Extra lessons:\n- On ~5 GB slices vLLM fails with `CUBLAS_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED` / \"No available memory for cache blocks\" unless `enforce_eager=True`, `max_num_batched_tokens≈512–1024`, `max_num_seqs≤48`, util≈0.09–0.095. 1–1.5B bf16 models and 3B-AWQ fit; 7–8B AWQ need ≥11 GB.\n- Large-vocab models (Gemma 256k) OOM in `prompt_logprobs`; cap batched tokens to 512. Pythia needs max_model_len ≤ 2048. kaitchup Phi-3 GPTQ: pass trust_remote_code=False (else offline fetch of remote code fails). google/gemma-3-1b-it snapshot lacks tokenizer → unusable.\n- LoRA (r=64, bs=2, len 320) of a 1.5B model trains in a 5 GB slice (~5–8 min/epoch for ~2.9k docs).\n- `pgrep -f \"<pattern>\"` inside a wait loop matches the loop's own bash command line → use `pgrep -f \"[x]pattern\"` bracket trick.\n- Qwen2.5-3B as a yes/no paraphrase-equivalence judge rejected ~90% of good paraphrases; embedding cosine (all-mpnet-base-v2, cached) worked better.\n- A retry loop's waiting must not count as failed tries (wrote gpu_run_wait.sh) — otherwise jobs give up while waiting for memory.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-19 (decoding_strategy_sensitivity workspace, 11 models × 132k gens, vLLM 0.32):\n- Killing a generate.py parent leaves its `VLLM::EngineCore` child orphaned (ppid 1) holding GPU memory — kill the EngineCore PID too.\n- Size budgets by KV/token, not weights: Phi-3-mini (no GQA) at 6 GB had a 2.2k-token KV cache → ~1 seq concurrency, useless. Olmo-3-7B is also KV-heavy.\n- A small idle slice (5–6 GB on a GPU at 0% util) beat a big slice on a GPU at 100% util shared with 4 jobs (Phi-3 GSM8K 12 min vs 40–80 min for 7B models on the busy GPU). Check `utilization.gpu`, not just free memory.\n- In generate scripts, size gpu_memory_utilization from `torch.cuda.mem_get_info()` minus ~1.2 GB (the parent's CUDA context counts), and bail if it's below ~75% of budget.\n- `trust_remote_code=True` + HF_HUB_OFFLINE fails for checkpoints whose config auto_map points to a Hub repo (kaitchup Phi-3 GPTQ); use False (native arch).\n- Qwen3-8B-AWQ (enable_thinking=False) works as a ~9 GB LLM judge: 17.6k rubric judgments in ~45 min, 0 parse failures.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-20 (tokenizer_fertility_bias workspace, encoder fine-tuning on ~5 GB slices):\n- No resource-finder output again; HF datasets with loading scripts (daily_dialog, banking77) fail — use parquet mirrors (`/api/datasets/<id>/parquet`) or the original CSV URLs in the script.\n- 110M–180M encoders fine-tune fine in 3–5 GB (bs32, len128, bf16 autocast, ~1 min/run). ByT5-small needs ~10 GB even on short sentences and >1 h per 256-word doc run under contention, and collapsed to majority class with Adafactor lr 1e-3 / 3 epochs — budget it as infeasible on shared slices. CANINE also collapsed on short docs in 4/7 runs.\n- Self-kill gotcha, bracket variant: `pgrep -f \"[r]un_queue.py ../logs/jobs_long\"` still killed the calling shell because the same Bash command later contained the literal text `run_queue.py ../logs/jobs_long.txt`. Find PIDs in one call, kill in a separate call.\n- statsmodels formula GLM with a bool outcome column models P(False) (patsy treats it as categorical) → flipped signs; cast to int.\n- Byte-level BPE (GPT-2/Llama/Qwen) offset_mapping includes the leading space; map tokens to words by the end offset.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-20 (icl_label_bias workspace): GLUE in the cache has only sst2 — RTE/MRPC parquet downloads directly from `huggingface.co/datasets/nyu-mll/glue/resolve/main/<task>/<split>-00000-of-00001.parquet`. One GPU was fully free at session start and got taken within ~5 min, so launch the biggest model immediately. On a busy shared GPU, 7–8B AWQ at ~8.6 GB budget (eager) ran ~10k 600-token prompts in ~75 min; 14B bf16 took 74 min. Two wait-loops seeing the same free GPU simultaneously race and OOM — stagger launches (~2 min). Bootstrap ECE CIs via pd.concat per resample were >10 min; per-item bin sufficient statistics × multinomial weights was seconds.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-20 (quantization_failure_modes workspace, 7 models × 7 RTN levels, vLLM 0.32):\n- In-place weight edits inside vLLM work: `llm.collective_rpc(fn)` with `VLLM_ALLOW_INSECURE_SERIALIZATION=1`, fn(self) touches `self.model_runner.model`; keep a CPU backup in the worker and quantize row-chunks (full-matrix fp32 temps OOM on slices). Disable prefix caching.\n- Don't call `torch.cuda.mem_get_info()` in the vLLM parent — its CUDA context eats ~0.8 GB on GPU0 and startup then fails the free-memory check; get total via nvidia-smi.\n- GPUs 0/1 top out at exactly 5125 MiB free (other users' 43 GB jobs) → budget ≤4.4 GB per slice. Multi-GPU TP rarely gets simultaneous slices; a queue with alternative configs (\"1:9.5,2:4.4\") was what worked. TP shards must keep quant groups aligned (Qwen2.5-3B intermediate 11008/4 not divisible by 128).\n- HF `device_map` sharding needs `accelerate` installed (not pulled in by vllm).\n- Self-kill again: `pkill -f`/`grep '[x]pat'` still matches if the SAME command line also contains the pattern elsewhere (e.g. a later nohup). Kill by PID in a separate call.\n- Contended-GPU timings: 7–8B bf16 on a 20 GB slice ~2–8 min/level for 1.6k prompts once GPU2 freed (~17:00–19:00); Phi-3 (KV-heavy) on 13 GB of a 100%-util GPU took 33 min.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_threshold workspace): GPUs saturated all session (≤6 GB free, 7–8B AWQ at 8 GB never started in 40+ tries). The user's own long-lived `vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --port 8011` (cascade venv, GPU3) was idle and usable via `/v1/completions` (raw prompt + stop strings; `stop_reason` returned) — check `curl 127.0.0.1:8011/v1/models` and `/metrics` num_requests_running before using; it gave the only 7B+ model and an LLM judge. For multi-round generation against a server, run each item's rounds in its own thread (synchronous batch rounds stall on stragglers: 4 min/round → 13 min total). New vLLM startup failure on contended GPUs: \"Error in memory profiling … other processes release GPU memory\" — just retry. HotpotQA distractor prompts reach ~2.4k tokens; use max_model_len ≥4096.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (context_window_decay workspace, long-context 1K–16K prompts):\n- GPUs saturated again from ~13:00 (other neurico sessions + liuxiao TP job); GPU0/1 \"5125 MiB free\" is another job's fluctuating activation memory — launches there fail even when the check passes. A single scheduler (one launch per GPU per 120 s) beats per-job wait loops, which raced each other (4 OOMs at once when GPU2 briefly freed 29 GB).\n- Startup-race fix that worked: in run_model.py, allocate a placeholder `torch.empty(budget+0.5 GB)` immediately, release it when the vLLM EngineCore child maps `libcuda.so` (poll /proc/<child>/maps every 50 ms). Guard with an env flag (HOLD_PARENT) — vLLM spawns EngineCore by re-importing __main__, so the hold code otherwise runs again in the child. Scheduler threshold: budget + 1.3 GB.\n- 16K-token prompts: Qwen2.5-7B-AWQ on a busy shared GPU ≈3k tok/s → 2,880 prompts (18M tokens) took 1h40; budget runs by total prompt tokens. 0.5–1.5B on an idle slice: 5–15 min.\n- `AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ` downloads in <1 min and runs in a ~4 GB slice up to ~9K context. Qwen3-0.6B (non-thinking) answers \"number or name\" questions with a number 95% of the time.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (cot_verbosity_tax workspace, 6+ concurrent neurico sessions): GPUs were ~fully oversubscribed; launches failed ~80% from free-memory races. Lessons:\n- Long multi-round generation (budget forcing, 12 rounds × up to 2k tokens) on a 100%-util shared GPU is very slow: 7B-AWQ forced arm took ~4 h incl. a ~60 min final answer pass (small KV → ~15 concurrent long contexts). Budget for it, or cut rounds/items for ≥7B.\n- My own jobs on the same GPU slow each other badly — don't stack 7B+14B+alt runs on one GPU.\n- Two of my queues polling independently launched on the same GPU in the same second and both failed: use ONE queue. A queue restart loses track of its running children → mark them (EXTERNAL file) or it relaunches duplicates. Stop leftover launch.sh wrappers at session end (they relaunch after you kill the child).\n- Qwen3-4B-AWQ (download ~2.5 GB) works as a 4.4 GB-slice LLM judge: ~1k rubric labels in ~4 min, 0 parse failures, but applies categories loosely — audit samples.\n- pandas 3: groupby().apply drops grouping columns — restore them by index join.\n- Background Bash with run_in_background still caps at 10 min when a timeout isn't given — set timeout explicitly for long waits.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (positional_retrieval_decay workspace): GPUs saturated again by ~6 parallel neurico sessions, each running retry loops that race for the same freed slices. What worked:\n- **Retry engine creation in-process** (poll nvidia-smi, size budget = min(max, free−0.35), retry LLM() after failures) instead of relaunching; build prompts AFTER engine init to shrink the race window.\n- **Work-stealing pool** (src/worker_pool.py): one thread per GPU launches the highest-priority job whose min budget fits; per-tag flock files prevent duplicates; exit code 75 = \"gave up waiting\" (not a failure). Chunked generation with append-only .partial files (whole samples) made kills/resumes safe and let analysis use partial runs.\n- Duplicate-run gotcha: a \"move finished .jsonl back to .partial\" extension script raced a queue that retried the same tag → two writers on one file. Use the lock in every code path.\n- 7–8B AWQ on a GPU shared by ~7 processes: 12–21 min per 720 prompts of avg 3K tokens; ≤3B models: 0.5–3 min. Budget large models at N/3.\n- WikiText-103 test+val gives only ~49 passages of ≥7.5K words (~10K tokens) → a \"16K\" haystack silently capped at ~10K; check ctx_tokens per length.\n- Single-needle retrieval is at ceiling for Qwen2.5 ≥1.5B / Llama-8B / Qwen3-4B up to 10K; near-miss distractors (same name, different type) are the discriminating condition.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-21 (prompt_surface_sensitivity workspace, 10 models × 24k prompts, vLLM 0.32):\n- Shared-GPU startup races were lost ~90% of the time (other sessions fill a freed slice during vLLM's ~20 s startup). What fixed it: TP=1 → `VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0` (in-process engine) + grab memory with a `torch.empty(budget)` tensor as soon as free, and monkeypatch `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker.MemorySnapshot` to free the tensor + `empty_cache()` just before vLLM measures. TP=2 → per-GPU holder subprocesses + `VLLM_WORKER_MULTIPROC_METHOD=fork` so forked workers inherit the patch and SIGKILL their GPU's holder. TP workers create CUDA ctx+NCCL (~0.8 GB) *before* the snapshot, so a 5.0 GB slice can't host TP; need ≥5.6 GB/GPU. Holders must exit when their parent dies (poll getppid), else orphans hog memory. Code: that workspace's src/generate.py, src/gpu_queue.py.\n- Llama-3.x and Gemma-2 chat templates `| trim` message content (trailing whitespace is a no-op); Qwen2.5 keeps it — useful as a free placebo.\n- vLLM greedy decoding is not batch-invariant: byte-identical prompts in different batch positions gave identical long (≤1024-tok) outputs only 23–76% of the time, moving IFEval accuracy by up to ~3 pp; short (≤10-tok) outputs ~99.7% identical.\n- Two GPU queues launching TP jobs onto the same GPU pair race each other — run one queue.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (sampling_temp_misinformation workspace, 7 models × 9 temps × n=10, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 was fully free at 11:20 and ~37 GB of it was taken within 5 min — launch immediately.\n- In-process hold (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0 + torch.empty placeholder + MemorySnapshot subclass patched in both\n  `vllm.utils.mem_utils` and `vllm.v1.worker.gpu_worker`) started 3B/7B-AWQ jobs on the first try. Re-acquire the hold\n  before each retry, or retries run without it and fail. Pick the GPU (max free) before importing torch, then pin to it.\n- Qwen2.5-3B bf16 at an 8.5 GB budget (non-eager, 4096 batched tokens) → \"No available memory for the cache blocks\".\n  3B-AWQ in 4.4 GB works.\n- SamplingParams(n=10, seed=s) seeds the samples consecutively → requests with seeds s and s+1 share 9/10 streams\n  (common random numbers across conditions). Space per-request seeds ≥ n apart if independence is wanted.\n- GSM8K 512-token CoT × 81 samples × 400 items on a 100%-util shared GPU: 1.5–3 h per ≤3B model; the QA part takes minutes.\n- Paired Wilcoxon on fractional, zero-heavy item-level diffs (sample-mean minus 0/1) contradicts the bootstrap CIs;\n  use a sign-flip permutation test of the mean.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-22 (embedding_drift_finetuning workspace, LoRA fine-tuning 160M–7B on shared GPUs):\n- A fully free GPU (48 GB) at session start was reservable with a tiny holder (`torch.empty(36GB)` + exit on a RELEASE file) while code was written; have the training script touch RELEASE after loading weights to CPU, just before `.cuda()`. But whatever you don't hold gets taken within minutes, and the 7B LoRA run (17 GB) still OOM'd mid-run when co-tenants grew — budget 7B training as infeasible unless a GPU is dedicated. On a GPU at 100% util shared with another user, 7B LoRA bs4×512 ran ~7 s/step; 3B 4.6 s/step; 1.5B 2.9 s/step; checkpoint evals (1.6k probes + 1,070 MCQ items) 4–8.5 min. 0.5B LoRA fits a 5 GB slice (2.4 GB peak, 20 min/3k steps); 1.5B peaks 4.5 GB.\n- \"Idle\" 5–6 GB slices at 0% util are transient: other users' 41–43 GB jobs regrow and OOM you minutes later.\n- Worker queue bug: a worker that retries the first unclaimed job starves smaller jobs if that job never fits → per-job memory need + skip; and when killing a worker loop, its job lock dir is orphaned (rmdir it). Writing reps before accuracy evals means a mid-eval kill leaves a checkpoint with reps but no metrics — filter to steps present in metrics.jsonl.\n- Science gotcha: always log held-out in-domain loss — Pythia-160M LoRA (r64, α128, lr 2e-4) diverged in every domain, and Qwen2.5 base on codeparrot Python showed no in-domain gain; \"drift\" results otherwise get misread.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (repetition_penalty_sideeffects workspace): GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:21 but was taken before vLLM was even installed (~10 min) — the uv install of vllm took >10 min (run it in background immediately). 7B-AWQ at 6.3–8.4 GB never started all session (all GPUs ≤1.5 GB free from 12:40); ≤3B AWQ at 4.2 GB ran fine. The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 had only ~2% KV usage at 24 concurrent requests — use 48–64 concurrency (~6 gen/s for 300-token outputs; LLM-judge ~75–100 calls/min). It accepts `repetition_penalty` and `chat_template_kwargs: {enable_thinking: false}` in /v1/chat/completions. HF `model.generate` with a custom LogitsProcessor (1–1.5B bf16, bs 50, 320 new tokens) took 5–45 s/batch on a shared slice — fine for ablations vLLM can't express.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (batch_size_inference_quality workspace, 10 configs × 1,700 prompts × 7 batch conditions, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 showed 48 GB free at 12:22 but a sibling session (3 neurico sessions start together at 12:00) took 40 GB within ~1 min — the free GPU at session start is a race you'll usually lose; plan for ~5 GB slices.\n- Killing a job to restart it with different settings loses its slice within seconds (other sessions' wait-loops grab it) — prefer env/file-driven behaviour changes decided at launch; unpinned (no CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES) relaunch with a smaller budget got a slice again.\n- Static-chunk batching in one engine (`llm.generate(chunk_of_B)`) is a clean batch-size manipulation. B=1 eager on a shared GPU: ~50–60 min (0.5–1.5B) for 1,700 prompts incl. 700 × ≤512-token CoT; 3B bf16 B=4 ~2 h → budget B=1 on subsets for ≥3B.\n- Result worth reusing: vLLM bf16 greedy is deterministic for an identical schedule (0 diffs) but batch-dependent: B=1 vs B≥4 changes 47–76% of CoT outputs; fp16 ~5× and fp32 ~12× fewer; divergences start at bf16 top-2 ties (logprob margin 0 or 0.125).\n- Analysis gotcha: iterating `set(a) & set(b)` of string ids gives PYTHONHASHSEED-dependent order → bootstrap CIs not reproducible; sort first.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-23 (system_prompt_saturation workspace, 9 models × 3.6k prompts up to 8k tokens, vLLM 0.32):\n- GPU2 fully free at 12:20; grabbed it with a chunked holder (1 GB tensors, target size read from a control file) — but\n  the 9 GB freed between two of my jobs was taken by other sessions within ~1 min, and later jobs got only 5–13 GB.\n- vLLM memory profiling charges other tenants' growth during startup to *your* \"non-torch\" usage (8B AWQ at 13 GB got\n  1.1 GB KV → startup ValueError). Fix: `LLM(kv_cache_memory_bytes=...)` skips profiling entirely; size the hold as\n  weights + KV + ~1.5 GB.\n- Holder handoff bugs: compute the holder's *actual* size from `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` for its PID, not the\n  target file; and on a 100%-full GPU, CUDA context creation raises `torch.AcceleratorError` (a RuntimeError), not\n  OutOfMemoryError — catch both and wait for the holder to free before touching CUDA.\n- Per-output-file `fcntl.flock` in generate.py (print \"LOCKED … 0 to do\" and exit 0) made several queues on different\n  GPUs safe against duplicate writers.\n- Timings on a GPU shared by ~8 processes: 8B AWQ with a 3.5 GB KV (28k tokens) needed 3 h for 3.6k prompts (1.2k of\n  them 1–8k tokens); 14B AWQ with 13.6k-token KV couldn't finish long prompts at all. Short-prompt experiments for\n  big models first, long-context ones only if KV ≥ ~20k tokens.\n- Greedy re-run of Qwen2.5-1.5B on a different GPU/KV size: 13% byte-identical texts but rule-check agreement 93.7%\n  and mean metric within 0.07 pp.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (lora_rank_blind_spots workspace, ~170 LoRA train+eval jobs of Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B, HF+peft, no vLLM):\n- GPUs had 0.5–6 GB free at 10:40, then ~25–43 GB freed on 3 GPUs at ~11:24 for ~10 min. A queue that only launches one job per GPU every 75 s wastes such a window; LAUNCH_GAP≈20–30 s filled it.\n- Without a concurrency cap my queue launched 52 jobs → load avg 60 on 32 cores, each job 5× slower. Cap running jobs (~15–20) and set OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 per job.\n- Mid-run OOMs from co-tenants: reserving memory at start (`torch.empty(RESERVE_GB)`, then del → it stays in PyTorch's cache) and saving the LoRA adapter right after training (so eval-only retries are possible) stopped most of the lost work. Real peak for a 0.5B LoRA job was 2.3–4.8 GB, more than estimated. HF generate prefill on 32 prompts × ~50 tokens still allocates ~0.5 GB of full-vocab logits.\n- After gradient_checkpointing_disable(), pass use_cache=True to generate explicitly.\n- Timings under contention: 0.5B LoRA, 250 steps, bs16 → 1.5 min (short classification inputs, idle GPU) up to 25–30 min (GSM8K/code, ~20 co-running jobs).\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (greedy_vs_nucleus_crossover workspace, 6 models × 6 tasks × 6 decoding conds, ~144k gens):\n- GPUs again ~4 GB free per GPU at 0% util. Qwen2.5-{0.5,1.5,3}B-Instruct-AWQ all ran in ≤3.7 GB slices (weights + kv 0.6–0.8 GB + ~1.0 GB overhead, `kv_cache_memory_bytes`, enforce_eager) and started first try; 7B-AWQ (7.4 GB) got a slice after ~35 min of waiting.\n- The user's `vllm serve Qwen3-8B` on :8011 was idle again: 8.1k chat requests (n=3, up to 512 tok) in ~25 min at 48 threads; single-token logprob judge (max_tokens=1, top_logprobs=20, probability-weighted 1–9) did 7.2k ratings in ~5–7 min. Digit mass ≈1.0.\n- Pooled-sample entropy conflates model errors with answer multiplicity (small models' wrong TriviaQA answers look \"high entropy\"); restrict to valid outputs for a gold-entropy proxy.\n- CPU sentence-embedding of ~40k texts under load avg ~50 took ~15 min; cache embeddings to disk for re-runs.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-26 (kv_cache_compression_artifacts workspace, HF-transformers 5.20 custom-attention work): no API key; Qwen3-8B server on :8011 again available as judge. GPUs at ~4 GB free at 10:40, then 0/1/2 freed to ~40 GB at ~11:24 for ~5 min (grab immediately; 7B bf16 at 16 GB later lost its slot during a worker hand-off — never kill a placed worker to \"restart with other args\"). Lessons:\n- Cache-policy experiments without vLLM: register a custom fn via `transformers.AttentionInterface`, run `model.model(..., use_cache=False, position_ids=...)` and keep your own KV store inside the fn (no mask needed: custom names skip HF mask creation). ~1 s/item for 1.5B at 6k ctx, 128-token chunks.\n- place_model pattern: load to CPU, poll nvidia-smi, `.cuda()` + warm-up forward + reserve; on failure `os._exit(75)` and relaunch from a bash loop (a failed in-process attempt leaves a 0.4 GB CUDA ctx pinned on that GPU).\n- Teacher-forced log_softmax over 256×152k vocab OOM'd a 4 GB slice mid-job → job lock orphaned with 4/150 rows. After a grid, check every output file's row count.\n- Qwen2.5 naive per-token 4-bit KV quant is catastrophic (key outlier channels); KIVI per-channel keys fine. Llama-3.2 fine either way.\n- LLM-judge for contradictions: free-form \"list contradictions\" with Qwen3-8B over-reports; slot extraction + deterministic comparison worked.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (tokenizer_merge_order workspace): **numpy ≥2 allocations >4 MB hang indefinitely** (100% CPU, e.g. `np.ones(5M uint16)`, `arr.astype(int64)`) because numpy madvises hugepages and THP defrag=madvise stalls on this host. Fix: `export NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0` for every python process (bytearray/torch allocs were fine). py-spy can't attach (no ptrace); use `python -X faulthandler` + `faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(40)`. GPUs 0–2 showed 48 GB free at 11:03 and were ~3 GB free by 11:08; GPU3 sat at 6.1 GB free / 0% util. Tiny-LM pretraining (23M params, ctx 512, bf16) ≈ 300k tok/s on an idle slice, ~2.5 GB at micro-batch 16.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-27 (gradient_masking_safety workspace, 14 models 0.5–32B × 5.4k prompts, vLLM 0.32): 3 GPUs were fully free at 11:03 and taken within ~10 min; grabbing 40 GB immediately with a 1-GB-chunk holder paid off. What worked best:\n- ONE long-lived in-process worker (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0) that runs all models sequentially and never returns memory to the driver: disable `torch.cuda.empty_cache`/`torch.accelerator.empty_cache`, drop placeholder chunks into PyTorch's cache at `MemorySnapshot.measure`, and add `memory_reserved()-memory_allocated()` to the snapshot's free_memory. Releasing to the driver at measure time lost ~9 GB to other tenants during weight loading (OOM). Queue entries `task=<fn>:<arg>` dispatched via importlib.reload(tasks) let code change without restarting (a restart = handoff race). Handoff to a new worker: TAKEOVER_PID tight-loop grab after the old pid exits kept 26/26 GB.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server intermittently returned CORRUPTED logprobs (sampled token != top-logprob argmax, top-k mass ~1e-6); it looked like a judge \"safety reflex\" (refusals scored as compliance, 9/37 accuracy). Validate every logprob judgment (argmax==sampled token, mass>=0.5, retry, fall back to text). With validation, ~10–19 judgments/s.\n- Judge design that validated (37 hand labels): reply-only \"does the assistant decline?\" (34/37) + request+reply \"does the reply contain content that carries out the request?\" (35/37). cais/HarmBench-Mistral-7b-val-cls weights downloaded to the cache (~14 GB, 3 min).\n- Timings on the shared A6000 (26 GB slice): ≤1.5B 2–4 min, 3B ~18 min, Phi-3 35 min, 7–9B 10–22 min, 14B-AWQ 8 min, 32B-AWQ 40 min per ~4–5k prompts (≤256 tokens), varying with contention.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (fewshot_example_contamination workspace, 8 models × 3,150 gens, vLLM 0.32): load avg 230–350; `ps aux`/`pgrep -af` hang on /proc of stuck processes — use `pgrep -x uv -u $USER` + /proc/<pid>/{stat,wchan,cwd} with `timeout`.\n- **uv install blocked for 10+ min**: 3 sibling sessions' `uv add` all waited on the shared ~/.cache/uv lock (futex) and copies cache(/ root FS)→/data. Fix: `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache uv sync` (same FS → hardlinks; fresh download of vllm stack ~3 min, \"Installed 215 packages in 7.8s\"). A killed uv became a zombie whose stuck threads still held `.venv/.lock` (find holder via `grep :<inode> /proc/locks`) → `mv .venv .venv_stuck_old` and recreate.\n- No-dep server client: `/usr/bin/python3 -I` + urllib works against :8011 while the venv installs.\n- Lane-script bug: `grep -q \"0 trials to do\"` matches \"3150 trials to do\" → failed launches counted as done. Anchor on `^<tag>: 0 trials to do`.\n- GPU0 was fully free 13:15–13:40; 7B/8B/14B AWQ all started first try with `kv_cache_memory_bytes` + gpu_memory_utilization=(budget+0.2)/48. :8011 Qwen3-8B did 3,150 short gens in ~25 min when idle, but crawled (~20/min) once other users loaded it to 110 concurrent requests.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (context_window_cliff workspace, long-context 1K–32K probes, vLLM 0.32): no API key again; `uv add vllm` took ~15 min (slow copy, disk 98%). :8011 Qwen3-8B server was shared with another session (~30–45 running reqs) yet did 1,548 prompts of avg 12K tokens in ~110 min at 16 threads. All my local jobs auto-landed on GPU0 (100% util) → 7B-AWQ ~3.9 prompts/min at 16K avg; moving 1.5B/3B AWQ to the *idle* GPU1 4.2 GB slice cut ETA ~8× (actual use ≈ weights+kv+0.5 GB, so OVERHEAD=0.5, MARGIN=0.25 fits). Shuffling prompt order before running makes any partial run a uniform subsample — kill slow jobs at the deadline without losing design balance. Science gotcha: Phi-3-mini-4k (kaitchup GPTQ) has sliding_window 2047 honoured by vLLM → retrieval of facts >2K tokens from the question ≈0% (looks like a \"length cliff\").\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (invisible_formatting_effects workspace, 11 models × 20k prompts, vLLM 0.32): `uv add vllm` took ~20 min (load avg 230–340, slow file copy) — start it first thing. GPU0 briefly had 18 GB free at 13:26 and then 20 GB at 14:49 — launching a worker immediately both times paid off. What worked:\n- **Worker hand-over without losing the slice** (to change code, queue order or registry): the new worker gets `TAKEOVER_PID=<old>`, finishes its imports, writes a ready file, busy-waits until /proc/<old> disappears, then creates its CUDA context and grabs 512 MB chunks. Kill the old PID in a *separate* call once the ready file exists. This kept 5/5 GB and 15/15 GB. It was used to move 14B code generation behind 7B/8B, triggered automatically by a log line (\"cls 14400/14400\").\n- Old workers re-read the shared queue file but keep their imported MODELS registry → adding new keys to that queue would KeyError them. Give new workers their own queue file.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server was fast at first (~3.5k short chat requests in 90 s), then other users loaded it (48 running + 63 waiting), and the last ~2.5k 512-token code requests took ~2 h.\n- Re-running the same checkpoint in a separate engine (same prompts, same shuffle) reproduced 19,968/19,968 outputs byte-identically; a byte-identical prompt at a different batch position changed 2–21% of 512-token code outputs but ~0.02% of short labels.\n- Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ on a 15 GB slice of a 100%-util GPU: ~5 min per 1.5k short prompts, ~13 min per 1.5k 512-token code prompts.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-30 (system_prompt_interference workspace, 7 models × 5.1k prompts, vLLM 0.32): GPU0 was fully free at 13:17 and taken (41 GB) within seconds of my holder starting — I got ~6 GB, enough for ≤3B AWQ (~3 min/model for 5k short prompts). :8011 Qwen3-8B idle: 5.1k chats (≤512 tok) in ~10 min at 48 threads. A 9.3 GB-free poller caught a 10 GB slice for 7B-AWQ ~15 min in.\n- **Never `mv` a results file a live worker is appending to** — /tmp is another FS, so the worker's later writes went to an unlinked inode (lost 3.1k 7B gens). Copy (`cp`) for scoring snapshots.\n- TAKEOVER_PID hand-over lost the slice 2/2 times on GPU0 today (other tenant grows into freed memory within ms); worked earlier on GPU0 from a plain holder. Treat hand-over as risky when the GPU's co-tenant is actively allocating.\n- Shuffled prompt order made the partial 7B run a uniform subsample, but paired designs (first/last, base/repeat) lose most pairs (p²) — for paired designs shuffle at the *pair/group* level instead.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-30 (system_prompt_interference, paper stage): before writing a paper, re-audit REPORT claims against raw gens; \"no false negatives\" was wrong (P_ahoy `_strip_title` missed same-line `<<Title>> Ahoy`, 509 false fails). neurips_2025.sty with `[final]` alone leaves `\\@trackname` undefined (add `\\providecommand` after the package line); math.tex has no `\\Tabref`. paper_examples/ held only a README.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-30 (embedding_collapse_repetition workspace, 18 models frozen embeddings, HF only, no vLLM): stack (torch/transformers/sentence-transformers, ~2 min with `UV_CACHE_DIR=$PWD/.uv_cache`; delete that 3.8 GB cache afterwards). GPU0 showed 48 GB free at 13:17 and was gone within ~8 min; feature extraction fits ~3–4 GB slices and took ~1–5 min per model × 26k short inputs.\n- Cache gaps: gpt2-xl, facebook/opt-125m and thenlper/gte-small have incomplete/split snapshots; `snapshot_download` a config-only snapshot then link weights from the other snapshot into a private folder rather than editing the shared cache.\n- `echo \"$(date) rc=$?\"` reports date's status, not the job's: capture `rc=$?` on its own line first (this hid failures as rc=0). Overwriting a running bash script with `cat >` is unsafe; kill the loop first.\n- Foreground Bash calls that exceed their timeout get moved to background and are killed 10 min later; use nohup for long analysis.\n- Science: mean-pooled \"collapse\" under repetition can be pure dilution. Always pool over original tokens only as a check, keep a length-matched non-repetitive control, and check clean top-PC share (GPT-2 final layer 48%, ModernBERT depth .75 84%) before trusting erank %.\n\nUpdate 2026-10-30 (temperature_calibration_gaps workspace, 12 runs × 6,204 cells × 4 samples, vLLM 0.32): GPU0 48 GB free at 13:17; an existing workspace venv's python can run a chunked holder within seconds while your own `uv add vllm` runs (~4 min with UV_CACHE_DIR local, `.uv_cache` then deletable).\n- The \"hand placeholders to vLLM via PyTorch's cache\" trick failed for large allocations here (14B bf16, 7B bf16): 512 MB cached blocks can't serve multi-GB KV/embedding allocations → OOM with GBs \"reserved but unallocated\"; `expandable_segments` didn't fix it. What worked: pop w+1 GB of placeholders into the cache at MemorySnapshot.measure, wrap `gw.Worker.initialize_from_config` to release the rest with the real empty_cache just before KV allocation (v0.32 uses vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_runner.py, so patching gpu_model_runner silently does nothing), then refill an activation margin. Use enforce_eager=True. Set gpu_memory_utilization=(w+0.3)/47.4 or the startup check can fail by 0.02 GB.\n- A second engine in one process sometimes dies with \"CUDA error: invalid argument\". Safest is one model per process: finishing worker spawns successor (TAKEOVER_PID=self), waits for its per-PID ready file, exits. A shared `takeover_ready` filename is ambiguous with several workers — I acted on the wrong worker's file.\n- Idle small slices beat big busy ones by >10×: 3B AWQ on a 5.5 GB slice of GPU3 did 6.2k cells in ~8 min vs 7B on 100%-util GPU0 ~45 min. Check GPU1/3 slices first.\n- `mv`/`ls` on logs can stall minutes under load (showed `-?????????`). Grepping big worker logs in a poll loop stalled my pipeline: grep only current small logs. Don't reuse a log glob matching old failed-run lines.\n- CUDA allocator OOM spam grew one log to 14 MB; a tight grab() retry loop writes thousands of lines.\n- TAKEOVER_PID=1 waits forever; use a nonexistent PID for \"no predecessor\".\n- Science: mpnet embeddings of word salad collapse together (semantic diversity falls above the coherence cliff, an artefact); the Qwen3-8B judge floors at ~3 on a 1–9 scale even for gibberish → use share ≥5. Mistral-7B AWQ (solidrust) scored only .27 on HumanEval while 0.72 on TriviaQA; not an import artefact.\n- analyze.py with 2,000 bootstraps × 12 models × many metrics takes ~10 min under load; run in background.\n","numLines":205,"startLine":1,"totalLines":205}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AyNYdvocVncXXt941eMFcm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
