{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZVWBVpBGLwK7ZXckZhn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The literature already contradicts parts of the hypothesis, which sharpens the design: Xie and Mori predict exponential batch-size scaling, and Zhou 2020 favors SGD over Adam. I'll write the literature review and preregister the plan with success criteria before running anything.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2152,"cache_read_input_tokens":85920,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2152},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b0894c3-d91e-45a5-b742-1e1609c0e675","uuid":"ae6823ed-3d8d-4c5a-9f21-b86b72749eda","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:02:44.774Z","request_id":"req_011CgZVWBGRncbc3dR7p1e86","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":2,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZVWBVpBGLwK7ZXckZhn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YVamxZia7cg8EBtQfSYM4u","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"pwd\ncat > literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n# Literature Review: Escape Times of Stochastic Optimizers from Loss Basins\n\n*Note: the resource-finding stage produced no output for this workspace. This review was compiled during\nthe research session. Every paper below had its arXiv metadata checked via the arXiv API on 2026-10-30;\nthe formulas quoted from Xie 2021, Mori 2022, Xie 2022 and Zhou 2020 were read from the PDFs in `papers/`.\nAn arXiv search for \"escape time\" + Weibull + \"stochastic gradient\" returned nothing, as did\n\"first passage\" + SGD + \"loss landscape\". That is evidence of a gap but not proof of one (arXiv full-text\nkeywords only).*\n\n## The theoretical frame: Kramers escape\nKramers (1940) gives the rate for overdamped Langevin dynamics at temperature T over a barrier ΔE:\nκ = (√(a|b|)/2π) exp(−ΔE/T), with a, b the curvatures at the well and saddle. At high barrier the escape is a\nPoisson process, so **first-passage times are exponential** (Weibull k = 1). Any deviation must come from outside\nthat limit: a relaxation transient, a low barrier, or a mixture of rates.\n\n## SGD as an SDE\n- **Mandt et al. 2017** and **Smith & Le 2018**, **Jastrzębski et al. 2018**: for SGD, the temperature scales\n  as T ∝ η/B. This is the origin of \"the ratio of batch size to learning rate controls temperature\".\n- **Zhu et al. 2019 (arXiv:1803.00195)**: SGD noise is anisotropic and aligned with sharp directions, which speeds escape\n  from sharp minima relative to isotropic noise.\n- **Xie et al. 2021 (arXiv:2002.03495)**: with noise covariance ≈ Hessian, mean escape time is exponential in\n  (B/η)·ΔL/H. \"The ratio of the batch size and the learning rate exponentially matters.\"\n- **Mori et al. 2022 (arXiv:2105.09557)**: with noise ∝ L(θ), the rate is a power law in the loss ratio,\n  κ ~ (√(h*|h_s|)/2π)·(L(θ_s)/L(θ*))^−(B/(ηh*) + 1 − n/2). Note it is a power law in **L(θ_s)/L(θ*)**, whereas\n  log τ remains **linear in B/η**.\n- **Ibayashi & Imaizumi 2023 (arXiv:2111.04004)**: exponential escape efficiency in a non-stationary regime without a\n  stationary-distribution assumption.\n- **Şimşekli et al. 2019 (arXiv:1901.06053)**: heavy-tailed gradient noise; Lévy jump models predict polynomial, not\n  exponential, dependence of escape time on barrier *height* and scale with basin *width*.\n\n**Implication for this project.** No theory above predicts τ ∝ B^{1/2}. Arrhenius-type theories predict\nlog τ linear in B/η, i.e. doubling-B factors that *grow with B*. A barrier-free diffusive first passage over a fixed\ndistance predicts τ ∝ B/η² (factor 2 per doubling). √2 corresponds to no standard regime and is therefore a substantive\nand falsifiable claim.\n\n## Adam vs SGD\n- **Zhou et al. 2020 (arXiv:2010.05627)**: Lévy-SDE analysis; Adam's escape time is larger than SGD's because Adam's\n  basin-measure is larger: \"SGD is more locally unstable\" at sharp minima. This **contradicts** H2's direction.\n- **Xie et al. 2022 (arXiv:2006.15815)**: \"Adaptive Inertia\": Adam's adaptive learning rate accelerates saddle\n  escape but *hurts* flat-minima selection relative to SGD; momentum helps with saddles, not minima selection.\n- No direct measurement of an Adam:SGD escape-time ratio split by basin width was found. Any such ratio depends on\n  how the two learning rates are calibrated: that confound has to be handled explicitly.\n\n## Saddles\n- **Jin et al. 2017 (arXiv:1703.00887)**, **Daneshmand et al. 2018 (arXiv:1803.05999)**: rate-of-escape results via\n  perturbation or intrinsic gradient noise; neither claims which negative eigendirection wins.\n- Linear theory (our derivation): near a saddle, the component along eigenvector i grows as\n  ~ z_i σ_i (1 + η|λ_i|)^t, with z_i Gaussian. So the most-negative direction wins *in probability*, with odds set by both\n  the eigenvalue gap (relative to the log-time of escape) and the noise projection σ_i. The gap alone is an incomplete\n  predictor, a testable refinement of H4.\n- **Fukumizu & Amari 2000**, **Şimşek et al. 2021 (arXiv:2105.12221)**: critical points by unit duplication and\n  symmetry give a source of true saddles in small networks.\n\n## Sharpness caveats\n- **Keskar et al. 2017** and **Dinh et al. 2017 (arXiv:1703.04933)**: sharpness is not reparametrization-invariant. We\n  report λ_max and trace as measured in a fixed parameterization, and use them descriptively only.\n\n## Metric conventions\n- Yaida 2019 (arXiv:1810.00004): fluctuation-dissipation relations, a check for stationarity of SGD.\n- Survival analysis: Weibull with interval censoring. AIC-based model selection is the standard way of comparing\n  Weibull, exponential, gamma and lognormal fits.\nEOF\ncat > resources.md <<'EOF'\n# Resources\n\nThe resource-finder stage produced nothing here, so all resources were gathered in this session.\n\n## Papers (`papers/`)\n| File | Paper |\n|---|---|\n| 1803.00195.pdf | Zhu et al., The Anisotropic Noise in SGD |\n| 2002.03495.pdf | Xie et al., A Diffusion Theory for Deep Learning Dynamics |\n| 2105.09557.pdf | Mori et al., Power-law Escape Rate of SGD |\n| 2111.04004.pdf | Ibayashi & Imaizumi, Exponential Escape Efficiency of SGD |\n| 2006.15815.pdf | Xie et al., Adaptive Inertia |\n| 2010.05627.pdf | Zhou et al., Towards Theoretically Understanding Why SGD Generalizes Better Than Adam |\n| 2105.12221.pdf | Şimşek et al., Geometry of the Loss Landscape in Overparameterized NNs |\n\n## Datasets\n- **UCI optdigits** (`sklearn.datasets.load_digits`): 1,797 8×8 images, 10 classes, bundled with scikit-learn.\n- **Fashion-MNIST** (`datasets/raw_fmnist/`): `train-images-idx3-ubyte.gz`, `train-labels-idx1-ubyte.gz` from\n  `http://fashion-mnist.s3-website.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/`. We use a 2,000-example subset, PCA-reduced.\n  See `datasets/README.md`.\n\n## Code\nNo baseline repositories were available or needed: `src/` is a from-scratch batched replica simulator.\n\n## API/LLM note\nThis study runs real SGD/Adam on real networks; no LLM is involved and no API keys were needed or present.\nEOF\ncat > datasets/README.md <<'EOF'\n# Datasets\n- `load_digits` ships with scikit-learn, no download.\n- Fashion-MNIST (not committed): \n  `curl -O http://fashion-mnist.s3-website.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/train-images-idx3-ubyte.gz` and\n  `train-labels-idx1-ubyte.gz`, into `datasets/raw_fmnist/`.\nEOF\ncat > planning.md <<'EOF'\n# Research Plan: Escape-Time Distributions of SGD and Adam\n\n## Motivation & Novelty Assessment\n\n### Why This Research Matters\nHow long an optimizer stays in a basin underlies theories of implicit regularization, flat-minima bias and LR/batch-size\nschedules. Those theories rest on *mean* escape-time formulae, derived asymptotically. The full distribution is what tells us\nwhether escape is memoryless (Poisson-like), and hence whether the SDE picture applies at practical noise levels.\n\n### Gap in Existing Work\nPrior work (Xie 2021, Mori 2022, Ibayashi 2023) measures mean rates. Distribution *shape* in real networks with censoring and\nmodel comparison appears unmeasured. Adam-vs-SGD escape is theorised with opposite signs (Zhou 2020 vs H2), with no measured\nratio by basin width. Which negative eigendirection SGD follows out of a true saddle is not measured against linear theory.\n\n### Our Novel Contribution\nA batched replica simulator with hundreds of independent runs from *the same* exact minimum/saddle; basin membership defined by\nquench; interval-censored distribution fits; decomposition of non-exponentiality into transient vs heterogeneity vs memoryless;\na calibration-robust Adam/SGD comparison; direct falsification tests of the specific numerical claims in the hypothesis.\n\n### Experiment Justification\n- E0 (controlled potentials with known Kramers rate): validates simulator and estimators against an exact answer, and maps which\n  mechanism yields k≠1. Without it, an NN \"k=0.9\" is uninterpretable.\n- E1 (NN minima): measures the shape parameter k per minimum vs geometry. Tests H1.\n- E2 (Adam vs SGD at narrow/wide minima, two LR calibrations). Tests H2.\n- E3 (batch/LR grid). Tests H3 including the rival Arrhenius form.\n- E4 (true saddles, exact Hessian). Tests H4 with chance and theory baselines.\n\n## Research Question\nWhat is the distribution of first-exit times of SGD/Adam from basins in small neural nets; and do its shape, optimizer dependence,\nbatch-size dependence, and saddle exit direction follow the four numerical claims below?\n\n## Hypothesis Decomposition (preregistered criteria, fixed before any experiment)\n- **H1a** Weibull fits better than exponential: LRT k=1 rejected (BH-FDR 5%) in a majority of NN conditions, ΔAIC>10.\n- **H1b** Shape range: per-condition k within [0.7, 1.3] in ≥90% of conditions, with both k<1 and k>1 present.\n- **H1c** Geometry dependence: |Spearman ρ(k, log sharpness)| significant across minima (p<.05).\n- **H2a** Narrow: τ_SGD/τ_Adam 95% CI inside [2,5] at matched-temperature calibration.\n- **H2b** Wide: ratio CI inside [0.67, 1.5].\n- **H2c** (calibration-free) ratio-of-ratios narrow/wide > 1.\n- **H3a** slope of log₂τ on log₂B CI contains 0.5 and excludes 0 and 1, on a constant-slope (power law) model.\n- **H3b** Power-law log τ~log B fits better than the Arrhenius log τ~B by AIC.\n- **H3c** Same η/B → same SDE time τ·η (within CI), for pairs (η,B),(2η,2B),(4η,4B).\n- **H4a** Pooled top-eigenvector exit accuracy CI overlaps [70,85]% and exceeds chance (1/index).\n- **H4b** Accuracy increases with eigenvalue gap (logistic slope > 0).\n\n## Proposed Methodology\n\n### Approach\nReal optimizers on real data. Tanh MLPs (smooth, exact Hessians) with L2 penalty on Fashion-MNIST/digits: underparameterized so minima\nare strict and per-example gradients nonzero. Each condition: R independent replicas start at the *same* polished minimum θ* (or saddle).\n\n**Escape definition** (primary): basin membership by quench: replicas are periodically relaxed with full-batch descent, then tested\nfor a loss barrier on the segment to θ*. Secondary (robustness): first passage of loss level L*+δ, and of radius ‖θ−θ*‖=R.\n\n### Experimental Steps\n1. E0. d-dim double well, finite-sum per-sample linear noise (additive) and loss-proportional noise; compare τ with Kramers.\n2. Minima: find ≥ 12 per dataset with varied protocols (init scale etc.); polish; exact Hessian.\n3. E1/E2/E3 grids (≥ 256 replicas per condition).\n4. E4: find saddles of index ≥ 2 with Newton on ‖∇L‖²; ≥ 100 SGD runs per saddle; project exit displacement on eigenvectors.\n\n### Baselines\nExponential (Kramers); Kramers rate on E0; chance (1/index) and linear-theory direction prediction on E4.\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\nWeibull k; AIC among exponential/Weibull/gamma/lognormal; mean/median τ (restricted mean where censored); slope; exit accuracy.\n\n### Statistical Analysis Plan\nInterval-censored MLE. Bootstrap CIs resampling *replicas*, and for cross-minimum summaries resampling *minima* (the replicas of one\nminimum are not independent draws of the population). BH-FDR for LRTs. α=.05. Censoring-robust time: median, or Weibull-implied mean.\nA mix of censored conditions is reported, never dropped.\n\n## Expected Outcomes\nTheory-based expectation: k≈1 at high barrier; k>1 from a start-up transient; k<1 from pooled heterogeneity; log τ linear in B. H3 is\nexpected to fail; H2 direction uncertain.\n\n## Timeline and Milestones\nPlanning 20m; simulator + pilot 45m; E0–E3 90m; E4 40m; analysis 40m; docs 30m.\n\n## Potential Challenges\n- No escapes within budget / immediate escapes (too low or too high T): pilot to choose T window; report the regime.\n- Replica non-independence across minima: bootstrap at minimum level.\n- Adam LR not commensurable with SGD's: two calibrations and H2c.\n- Shared GPUs: replicas fit a 1–3 GB slice; CPU fallback.\n\n## Success Criteria\nAll four sub-hypotheses receive a clear verdict with CIs, simulator validated against Kramers on E0, reported irrespective of direction.\nEOF\ngit -C . log --oneline -1 2>/dev/null | head -1; git rev-parse --show-toplevel","description":"Write literature review, resources and preregistered plan"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2152,"cache_read_input_tokens":85920,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2152},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b0894c3-d91e-45a5-b742-1e1609c0e675","uuid":"1a3f89c6-6132-4076-9048-c162106e02fd","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:03:33.430Z","request_id":"req_011CgZVWBGRncbc3dR7p1e86","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01YVamxZia7cg8EBtQfSYM4u":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01 && pwd\ncat > literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n# Literature Review: Escape Times of Stochastic Optimizers from Loss Basins\n\n*Note: the resource-finding stage produced no output for this workspace. This review was compiled during\nthe research session. Every paper below had its arXiv metadata checked via the arXiv API on 2026-10-30;\nthe formulas quoted from Xie 2021, Mori 2022, Xie 2022 and Zhou 2020 were read from the PDFs in `papers/`.\nAn arXiv search for \"escape time\" + Weibull + \"stochastic gradient\" returned nothing, as did\n\"first passage\" + SGD + \"loss landscape\". That is evidence of a gap but not proof of one (arXiv full-text\nkeywords only).*\n\n## The theoretical frame: Kramers escape\nKramers (1940) gives the rate for overdamped Langevin dynamics at temperature T over a barrier ΔE:\nκ = (√(a|b|)/2π) exp(−ΔE/T), with a, b the curvatures at the well and saddle. At high barrier the escape is a\nPoisson process, so **first-passage times are exponential** (Weibull k = 1). Any deviation must come from outside\nthat limit: a relaxation transient, a low barrier, or a mixture of rates.\n\n## SGD as an SDE\n- **Mandt et al. 2017** and **Smith & Le 2018**, **Jastrzębski et al. 2018**: for SGD, the temperature scales\n  as T ∝ η/B. This is the origin of \"the ratio of batch size to learning rate controls temperature\".\n- **Zhu et al. 2019 (arXiv:1803.00195)**: SGD noise is anisotropic and aligned with sharp directions, which speeds escape\n  from sharp minima relative to isotropic noise.\n- **Xie et al. 2021 (arXiv:2002.03495)**: with noise covariance ≈ Hessian, mean escape time is exponential in\n  (B/η)·ΔL/H. \"The ratio of the batch size and the learning rate exponentially matters.\"\n- **Mori et al. 2022 (arXiv:2105.09557)**: with noise ∝ L(θ), the rate is a power law in the loss ratio,\n  κ ~ (√(h*|h_s|)/2π)·(L(θ_s)/L(θ*))^−(B/(ηh*) + 1 − n/2). Note it is a power law in **L(θ_s)/L(θ*)**, whereas\n  log τ remains **linear in B/η**.\n- **Ibayashi & Imaizumi 2023 (arXiv:2111.04004)**: exponential escape efficiency in a non-stationary regime without a\n  stationary-distribution assumption.\n- **Şimşekli et al. 2019 (arXiv:1901.06053)**: heavy-tailed gradient noise; Lévy jump models predict polynomial, not\n  exponential, dependence of escape time on barrier *height* and scale with basin *width*.\n\n**Implication for this project.** No theory above predicts τ ∝ B^{1/2}. Arrhenius-type theories predict\nlog τ linear in B/η, i.e. doubling-B factors that *grow with B*. A barrier-free diffusive first passage over a fixed\ndistance predicts τ ∝ B/η² (factor 2 per doubling). √2 corresponds to no standard regime and is therefore a substantive\nand falsifiable claim.\n\n## Adam vs SGD\n- **Zhou et al. 2020 (arXiv:2010.05627)**: Lévy-SDE analysis; Adam's escape time is larger than SGD's because Adam's\n  basin-measure is larger: \"SGD is more locally unstable\" at sharp minima. This **contradicts** H2's direction.\n- **Xie et al. 2022 (arXiv:2006.15815)**: \"Adaptive Inertia\": Adam's adaptive learning rate accelerates saddle\n  escape but *hurts* flat-minima selection relative to SGD; momentum helps with saddles, not minima selection.\n- No direct measurement of an Adam:SGD escape-time ratio split by basin width was found. Any such ratio depends on\n  how the two learning rates are calibrated: that confound has to be handled explicitly.\n\n## Saddles\n- **Jin et al. 2017 (arXiv:1703.00887)**, **Daneshmand et al. 2018 (arXiv:1803.05999)**: rate-of-escape results via\n  perturbation or intrinsic gradient noise; neither claims which negative eigendirection wins.\n- Linear theory (our derivation): near a saddle, the component along eigenvector i grows as\n  ~ z_i σ_i (1 + η|λ_i|)^t, with z_i Gaussian. So the most-negative direction wins *in probability*, with odds set by both\n  the eigenvalue gap (relative to the log-time of escape) and the noise projection σ_i. The gap alone is an incomplete\n  predictor, a testable refinement of H4.\n- **Fukumizu & Amari 2000**, **Şimşek et al. 2021 (arXiv:2105.12221)**: critical points by unit duplication and\n  symmetry give a source of true saddles in small networks.\n\n## Sharpness caveats\n- **Keskar et al. 2017** and **Dinh et al. 2017 (arXiv:1703.04933)**: sharpness is not reparametrization-invariant. We\n  report λ_max and trace as measured in a fixed parameterization, and use them descriptively only.\n\n## Metric conventions\n- Yaida 2019 (arXiv:1810.00004): fluctuation-dissipation relations, a check for stationarity of SGD.\n- Survival analysis: Weibull with interval censoring. AIC-based model selection is the standard way of comparing\n  Weibull, exponential, gamma and lognormal fits.\nEOF\ncat > resources.md <<'EOF'\n# Resources\n\nThe res